const TOPICSTORIES = [
{"id":"silk-road","title":"The Silk Road","sub":"Silk, paper, faiths and plague across Asia, 138 BCE to 1498","stops":[
 {"t":"138 BCE: A Han envoy goes west","text":"In 138 BCE the Han emperor Wu sent Zhang Qian west to find allies against the Xiongnu nomads. He was captured, escaped, and came home thirteen years later with no allies. He did bring news of rich cities beyond the mountains, in Ferghana and Bactria, and of fine horses. The Han court began sending caravans west along the road on the map.","y":-138,"r":["ea","ca"],"l":[],"item":"Zhang Qian sets out west","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Zhang Qian","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhang_Qian"},"m":[{"p":[[34.3,108.9],[40.1,94.7],[39.5,76.0],[40.4,71.8],[36.8,66.9]],"t":"Zhang Qian's road west"}]},
 {"t":"100 CE: Four empires, many hands","text":"By 100 CE four empires lined the road: Han China, the Kushans, Parthia and Rome. Nobody travelled the whole way. Goods passed from trader to trader, and each one added to the price. The Kushans sat in the middle and grew rich. Romans paid so much for Chinese silk that under the emperor Tiberius the Senate banned men from wearing it.","y":100,"r":["ea","ca","me","eu"],"l":["trade"],"item":"Kushan Empire","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Silk Road","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk_Road"},"m":[{"p":[[34.3,108.9],[40.1,94.7],[39.5,76.0],[36.8,66.9],[37.66,62.19],[33.09,44.58],[36.2,36.16]],"t":"Silk goes west"}]},
 {"t":"313: A lost postbag","text":"The traders who ran the road were Sogdians, from the cities around Samarkand. About 313 a bag of their letters was lost near Dunhuang, and it was found in 1907. One merchant reports that the Chinese capital has burned and the emperor has fled. In another letter a wife, left behind without money, writes angrily to her husband.","y":313,"r":["ca","ea"],"l":["trade"],"item":"Sogdian Ancient Letters","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Sogdian Ancient Letters","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sogdian_Ancient_Letters"},"m":[{"p":[[39.65,66.96],[39.5,76.0],[40.1,94.7],[34.62,112.45]],"t":"Sogdian trade"}]},
 {"t":"645: A monk comes home with books","text":"Ideas moved along the road with the goods, and Buddhism came into China this way. In 629 the monk Xuanzang slipped out of Tang China and travelled to India to study at Nalanda. He came home in 645 with 657 texts, and spent the rest of his life translating them. The arrow shows his way out.","y":645,"r":["ea","ca","sa"],"l":["religion"],"item":"Xuanzang","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Xuanzang","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xuanzang"},"m":[{"p":[[34.3,108.9],[40.1,94.7],[42.95,89.18],[39.65,66.96],[36.8,66.9],[34.83,67.83],[34.0,71.5],[25.14,85.44]],"t":"Xuanzang to India"}]},
 {"t":"751: Paper goes west","text":"In 751 an Abbasid army beat a Tang army at the Talas River. Tradition says Chinese prisoners taken there taught papermaking in Samarkand. Whether or not that is true, paper mills appear in Samarkand and then in Baghdad soon after. Paper was far cheaper than parchment. It carried the books of the Islamic world for centuries before it reached Europe.","y":751,"r":["ca","me","ea","eu"],"l":["ideas"],"item":"Papermaking at Samarkand","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: History of paper","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_paper"},"m":[{"p":[[42.52,72.23],[39.65,66.96],[33.31,44.36],[30.04,31.24],[34.03,-5.0],[38.99,-0.52]],"t":"Paper moves west"}]},
 {"t":"1271: One empire, one road","text":"In the 1200s the Mongols conquered most of the road, from China to Persia. Under one power, with guarded relay posts and passes for travellers, crossing Asia became safer than it had ever been. Marco Polo left Venice in 1271 and reached the court of Kublai Khan about four years later. His book told Europeans how rich China was.","y":1271,"r":["eu","me","ca","ea"],"l":[],"item":"Marco Polo travels to China","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Marco Polo","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marco_Polo"},"m":[{"p":[[45.44,12.33],[32.93,35.08],[38.08,46.29],[27.1,56.45],[36.8,66.9],[39.5,76.0],[40.1,94.7],[42.36,116.18]],"t":"Marco Polo, 1271 to 1275"}]},
 {"t":"1346: The road carries plague","text":"The same roads carried disease. Plague killed people near Lake Issyk-Kul in 1338 and 1339. By 1346 it had reached Caffa on the Black Sea, and in 1347 ships carried it to Constantinople, Sicily and Marseille. In five years it killed between a third and a half of the people of Europe, and a great many in the Middle East.","y":1346,"r":["ca","eu","me"],"l":["disaster"],"item":"Black Death","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Black Death","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Death"},"m":[{"p":[[42.4,77.2],[48.5,45.0],[45.03,35.38]],"t":"Plague moves west"},{"p":[[45.03,35.38],[41.01,28.98],[38.19,15.55],[43.3,5.37]],"t":"By ship, 1347"}]},
 {"t":"1498: The sea takes over","text":"The land road never fully recovered. The Mongol empire broke up, and travel grew costly and unsafe again. Europeans looked for a way round. In 1498 Vasco da Gama sailed round Africa to Calicut in India. Ships could carry far more than camels, and within a century most trade between Europe and Asia went by sea.","y":1498,"r":["eu","af","sa"],"l":[],"item":"Vasco da Gama reaches Calicut","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Vasco da Gama","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vasco_da_Gama"},"m":[{"p":[[38.72,-9.14],[15.0,-23.5],[-20.0,-25.0],[-34.36,18.47],[-3.22,40.12],[11.25,75.78]],"t":"Da Gama, 1497 to 1498"}]}]},
{"id":"voc","title":"The Dutch East India Company","sub":"Spices, shares and force across Asia, 1602 to 1799","stops":[{"t":"1602: A company anyone could own","text":"In 1602 the Dutch government merged rival trading firms into one company, the VOC. Only it could trade with Asia for the Dutch, and it could also build forts, sign treaties and fight wars. Anyone could buy a share and sell it on in Amsterdam, and that money paid for the long voyages you can follow round Africa on the map.","y":1602,"r":["eu","se"],"l":["trade"],"item":"Amsterdam Exchange","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Dutch East India Company","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_East_India_Company"},"m":[{"p":[[52.37,4.9],[53.0,4.75],[51.2,1.8],[50.0,-2.5],[48.0,-7.0],[39.0,-11.5],[28.5,-16.5],[15.0,-24.5],[0.0,-24.0],[-15.0,-27.0],[-28.0,-18.0],[-34.0,8.0],[-34.36,18.47]],"t":"Round Africa to Asia"}]},{"t":"1619: Batavia, hub of Asian trade","text":"In 1619 Jan Pieterszoon Coen destroyed the Javanese port of Jayakarta and built Batavia, today's Jakarta, on its ruins. Ships from Europe reached it by the Brouwer route, running east on strong winds far south of Africa, then turning north to Java. Batavia became the hub of a web of trade inside Asia, where Indian cloth was swapped for spices, as the arrows show.","y":1619,"r":["se","sa","eu"],"l":["trade","infra"],"item":"Batavia","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Batavia, Dutch East Indies","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batavia,_Dutch_East_Indies"},"m":[{"p":[[-34.36,18.47],[-38.5,30.0],[-39.0,55.0],[-37.83,77.55],[-28.0,95.0],[-12.0,104.0],[-6.3,105.6],[-6.13,106.81]],"t":"Brouwer route to Java"},{"p":[[13.42,80.32],[10.0,81.6],[7.0,82.6],[5.6,88.0],[5.9,95.0],[4.0,99.0],[2.0,102.0],[1.2,104.2],[-2.0,106.0],[-3.5,106.9],[-6.0,106.85]],"t":"Cloth from India"},{"p":[[-3.7,128.18],[-4.6,125.0],[-6.2,120.0],[-5.6,113.0],[-5.9,108.5],[-6.05,106.85]],"t":"Cloves from Ambon"}]},{"t":"1621: Banda and the nutmeg monopoly","text":"Nutmeg and mace grew only on the tiny Banda Islands, and the Bandanese kept selling to other buyers. In 1621 Coen's forces conquered the islands, and of about 15,000 people only around 1,000 were left, the rest killed, starved, enslaved or driven out. The company handed the land to Dutch planters whose groves were worked by enslaved people, and so it won its spice monopoly.","y":1621,"r":["se","eu"],"l":["trade","migration"],"item":"Dutch conquest of Banda","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Dutch conquest of the Banda Islands","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_conquest_of_the_Banda_Islands"},"m":[{"p":[[-6.0,106.85],[-5.8,112.0],[-6.6,118.0],[-6.0,124.5],[-4.9,128.5],[-4.53,129.9]],"t":"Coen's fleet from Batavia"},{"p":[[-4.53,129.9],[-5.2,127.0],[-6.3,122.0],[-6.4,116.0],[-5.6,110.0],[-6.05,106.85]],"t":"Captives taken to Batavia"}]},{"t":"Taiwan and Japan: silk for silver","text":"In 1624 the company built Fort Zeelandia on Taiwan, where it bought Chinese silk to sell in Japan. From 1641 Japan let the Dutch trade only from Dejima, a tiny island at Nagasaki, and for over 200 years they were the only Europeans allowed in. Koxinga's army took Taiwan in 1662, but Japanese silver and copper kept flowing south to Batavia.","y":1641,"r":["ea","se"],"l":["trade","infra"],"item":"Dejima","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Dejima","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dejima"},"m":[{"p":[[24.45,118.1],[23.6,119.2],[23.0,120.1],[24.5,122.4],[27.5,124.5],[31.0,128.0],[32.74,129.87]],"t":"Chinese silk via Taiwan"},{"p":[[32.74,129.87],[30.0,126.5],[25.5,120.6],[22.0,118.5],[15.0,113.5],[8.0,109.5],[2.0,106.0],[-3.0,106.6],[-6.05,106.85]],"t":"Silver and copper south"}]},{"t":"1652: A halfway station at the Cape","text":"In 1652 Jan van Riebeeck set up a station at the Cape of Good Hope to grow food for passing ships. Settlers' farms spread inland onto Khoikhoi grazing land, which led to wars and cost the Khoikhoi their land and herds. From 1658 the company brought in enslaved people, from West Africa, Madagascar and Asia, and the Cape came to run on their work.","y":1652,"r":["af","se","eu"],"l":["trade","migration"],"item":"Dutch settle the Cape","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Dutch Cape Colony","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_Cape_Colony"},"m":[{"p":[[-33.92,18.42],[-33.93,18.86],[-33.29,19.14],[-33.0,21.5],[-32.25,24.53]],"t":"Farms spread inland"},{"p":[[-17.0,43.8],[-22.0,42.0],[-28.0,36.0],[-33.5,28.0],[-35.0,21.0],[-34.4,18.6],[-33.92,18.42]],"t":"Captives from Madagascar"}]},{"t":"Ceylon: cinnamon by force","text":"By 1658 the company had pushed the Portuguese off the coasts of Ceylon, now Sri Lanka, while the kingdom of Kandy kept the hills. Men of the Salagama caste were made to peel cinnamon for the company as a duty, and smuggling was harshly punished. With almost all the world's cinnamon in its hands, the company set the price and shipped it home round the Cape, as the arrow shows.","y":1658,"r":["sa","se","af"],"l":["trade","society"],"item":"Dutch Ceylon and cinnamon","src":{"t":"JSTOR Daily: Plant of the Month, Cinnamon","u":"https://daily.jstor.org/plant-of-the-month-cinnamon/"},"m":[{"p":[[6.93,79.85],[6.03,80.22],[0.0,74.0],[-15.0,60.0],[-28.0,42.0],[-34.8,25.0],[-34.36,18.47]],"t":"Cinnamon to Europe"}]},{"t":"A trade in people","text":"Between 660,000 and 1.1 million enslaved people were shipped through the company's ports around the Indian Ocean, from India, Bengal, Bali, Sulawesi and Madagascar. In Batavia they worked in houses, workshops and fields, and by the 1700s they made up a large share of the city. Follow the arrows to see where captives were taken from.","y":1700,"r":["se","sa","af"],"l":["migration","trade"],"item":"Slave trade in Dutch Asia","src":{"t":"African Studies Centre Leiden: Indian Ocean slave trade","u":"https://ascleiden.nl/content/webdossiers/indian-ocean-slave-trade"},"m":[{"p":[[20.5,92.5],[16.0,94.0],[8.0,96.5],[4.0,99.5],[2.0,102.0],[1.2,104.2],[-2.0,106.0],[-3.5,106.9],[-6.0,106.85]],"t":"Captives from Bengal"},{"p":[[-5.13,119.41],[-6.6,117.0],[-8.0,115.5],[-6.4,112.0],[-5.9,108.5],[-6.05,106.85]],"t":"Captives from Sulawesi, Bali"},{"p":[[-6.13,106.81],[-6.3,105.6],[-12.0,96.0],[-24.0,65.0],[-32.0,38.0],[-34.8,25.0],[-34.36,18.47]],"t":"Captives sent to the Cape"}]},{"t":"1799: Debt and the end of the VOC","text":"Smuggling, corruption among its own staff and strong British rivals ate into profits, and the war with Britain from 1780 to 1784 cost it ships, ports and trade. It borrowed to keep going, sank deep into debt, and was taken over by the Dutch state in 1796. Its charter ran out on 31 December 1799, after Britain had already seized the Cape and Ceylon, as the arrows show.","y":1799,"r":["eu","af","sa","se"],"l":["trade"],"item":"Fourth Anglo-Dutch War","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Dutch East India Company","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_East_India_Company"},"m":[{"p":[[50.3,-4.1],[48.0,-7.0],[39.0,-12.0],[28.5,-17.0],[15.0,-24.5],[0.0,-24.0],[-15.0,-27.0],[-28.0,-18.0],[-34.0,8.0],[-34.1,18.45]],"t":"British take the Cape, 1795"},{"p":[[13.08,80.29],[10.5,81.0],[8.57,81.3],[6.5,82.0],[5.8,80.7],[6.93,79.85]],"t":"British take Ceylon, 1796"}]}]},
{"id":"mongols","title":"The Mongol conquests","sub":"From one steppe people to the largest land empire, 1206 to 1368","stops":[
 {"t":"1206: Temüjin becomes Genghis Khan","text":"In 1206 the peoples of the Mongolian steppe met by the Onon River and named Temüjin their ruler, Genghis Khan. He had spent twenty years beating or absorbing his rivals. He broke up the old tribes and sorted the men into units of ten, a hundred and a thousand, led by men he chose for skill and loyalty.","y":1206,"r":["ca","ea"],"l":[],"item":"Temüjin proclaimed Genghis Khan","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Genghis Khan","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genghis_Khan"}},
 {"t":"1219: The fall of Khwarazm","text":"In 1218 a Khwarazmian governor killed a Mongol trade caravan at Otrar, and the shah then killed an envoy. Genghis Khan invaded in 1219. Bukhara, Samarkand, Merv and Nishapur fell within three years, and cities that resisted were destroyed. One of the richest regions of the Islamic world was left in ruins.","y":1219,"r":["ca","me"],"l":["war"],"item":"Khwarazmian Empire","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Mongol invasion of the Khwarazmian Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mongol_invasion_of_the_Khwarazmian_Empire"},"m":[{"p":[[47.2,102.84],[42.85,68.3],[39.77,64.42],[39.65,66.96],[37.66,62.19],[36.21,58.8]],"t":"Genghis Khan, 1219 to 1221"}]},
 {"t":"1235: A capital and a post system","text":"Genghis Khan died in 1227. His son Ögedei built a capital at Karakorum and set up the yam, a chain of relay stations with fresh horses and food. A messenger who changed horses at each one could cover well over a hundred miles in a day. The stations tied together an empire that already ran from north China to the Caspian Sea.","y":1235,"r":["ca","ea"],"l":["infra"],"item":"Karakorum","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Yam (route)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yam_(route)"},"m":[{"p":[[47.2,102.84],[44.2,80.4],[42.85,68.3],[48.5,45.0]],"t":"Relay road west"},{"p":[[47.2,102.84],[39.9,116.4]],"t":"To north China"}]},
 {"t":"1241: Into Europe","text":"Ögedei sent his nephew Batu west. Between 1237 and 1240 the Mongols burned Ryazan, Vladimir and Kiev. In April 1241 they beat a Polish army at Legnica and a Hungarian army at Mohi, two days apart. Then Ögedei died, and Batu pulled back to the Volga. There his line ruled as the Golden Horde.","y":1241,"r":["ca","eu"],"l":["war"],"item":"Batu Khan","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Mongol invasion of Europe","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mongol_invasion_of_Europe"},"m":[{"p":[[51.5,46.0],[54.6,39.7],[56.13,40.4],[50.45,30.52],[51.21,16.16]],"t":"Batu, 1237 to 1241"},{"p":[[50.45,30.52],[47.97,20.9]],"t":"Mohi"}]},
 {"t":"1258: Baghdad falls","text":"In 1258 Hulagu, a grandson of Genghis Khan, took Baghdad after a short siege. The caliph was killed, and the Abbasid caliphate ended after five centuries. Two years later an Egyptian Mamluk army beat a Mongol force at Ain Jalut in Palestine. It was the first major Mongol defeat, and it marked the limit of the empire in the west.","y":1258,"r":["me","ca"],"l":["war"],"item":"Mongols sack Baghdad","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Siege of Baghdad","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Baghdad"},"m":[{"p":[[38.08,46.29],[33.31,44.36]],"t":"Hulagu, 1258"},{"p":[[33.31,44.36],[36.2,37.16],[33.51,36.29],[32.55,35.36]],"t":"To Ain Jalut, 1260"}]},
 {"t":"1279: Kublai Khan rules all China","text":"Kublai Khan, another grandson, moved the capital to what is now Beijing and in 1271 took a Chinese dynasty name, Yuan. In 1279 his fleet destroyed the last Song forces, and for the first time outsiders ruled all of China. Two invasions of Japan, in 1274 and 1281, failed. A storm wrecked most of the second fleet.","y":1279,"r":["ea","ca"],"l":["war"],"item":"Kublai Khan","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Kublai Khan","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kublai_Khan"},"m":[{"p":[[39.9,116.4],[32.0,112.1],[30.25,120.17],[22.3,113.07]],"t":"Conquest of the Song"},{"p":[[35.2,128.57],[33.6,130.4]],"t":"Japan, 1274 and 1281"}]},
 {"t":"1300: Four khanates, open roads","text":"By 1300 the empire had split into four: the Yuan in China, the Chagatai khanate in Central Asia, the Ilkhanate in Persia and the Golden Horde on the western steppe. They fought each other at times, but trade crossed all four. Persian astronomers and doctors served in China, and Chinese styles shaped painting in Persia.","y":1300,"r":["ca","ea","me","eu"],"l":[],"item":"Ilkhanate","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Pax Mongolica","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pax_Mongolica"}},
 {"t":"1368: The Ming drive out the Yuan","text":"The khanates did not last. Plague, floods and famine hit Yuan China in the 1340s and 1350s, and rebellions spread. In 1368 the armies of Zhu Yuanzhang, a former monk turned rebel leader, took Beijing, and he founded the Ming dynasty. The Yuan court fled to the steppe. In the west, Russian princes paid tribute to the Golden Horde until 1480.","y":1368,"r":["ea","ca"],"l":[],"item":"Ming","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Ming dynasty","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_dynasty"},"m":[{"p":[[32.06,118.8],[39.9,116.4]],"t":"Ming armies, 1368"},{"p":[[39.9,116.4],[47.2,102.84]],"t":"The Yuan court flees"}]}]},
{"id":"buddhism","title":"How Buddhism spread","sub":"From the Ganges plain across Asia, 450 BCE to 1200","stops":[
 {"t":"About 450 BCE: The Buddha teaches","text":"Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, taught in the towns of the Ganges plain. The dates of his life are uncertain, and many scholars now place his death near 400 BCE. He taught that suffering comes from craving, and that a disciplined middle way ends it. After his death his followers kept the teaching alive by reciting it together.","y":-450,"r":["sa"],"l":["religion"],"item":"The Buddha teaches","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: The Buddha","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Buddha"},"m":[{"p":[[27.47,83.28],[24.7,84.99],[25.38,83.02],[26.74,83.89]],"t":"Birth, awakening, first sermon, death"}]},
 {"t":"260 BCE: An emperor takes it up","text":"About 260 BCE the Mauryan emperor Ashoka conquered Kalinga. The war was so bloody that, his own inscriptions say, he turned to the Buddha's teaching. He had edicts carved on rocks and pillars across India urging kindness and restraint. Tradition says he sent missions abroad, and that his son Mahinda carried Buddhism to Sri Lanka.","y":-260,"r":["sa"],"l":["religion"],"item":"Ashoka","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Ashoka","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashoka"},"m":[{"p":[[25.61,85.14],[8.31,80.4]],"t":"Mahinda to Sri Lanka"},{"p":[[25.61,85.14],[33.75,72.82]],"t":"Missions to the north-west"}]},
 {"t":"68 CE: Along the Silk Road to China","text":"Monks and merchants carried Buddhism north-west into Gandhara and the Kushan lands, then along the Silk Road. Tradition dates its arrival at the Han court to about 68 CE. For centuries it stayed a foreign faith in China. It grew after the Han fell, when translators such as Kumarajiva put the texts into clear Chinese.","y":68,"r":["sa","ca","ea"],"l":["religion"],"item":"Buddhism reaches China","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Silk Road transmission of Buddhism","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk_Road_transmission_of_Buddhism"},"m":[{"p":[[33.75,72.82],[34.83,67.83],[36.8,66.9],[39.5,76.0],[41.72,82.96],[40.1,94.7],[34.62,112.45]],"t":"Along the Silk Road"}]},
 {"t":"372: Korea, then Japan","text":"From China the faith went on to Korea. The kingdom of Goguryeo received monks in 372, and Baekje in 384. In the 500s a king of Baekje sent a Buddha image and texts to the Japanese court; the usual dates given are 538 or 552. Japanese rulers soon built temples, and Buddhism lived there beside the older worship of the kami.","y":372,"r":["ea"],"l":["religion"],"item":"Buddhism reaches Korea","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Korean Buddhism","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Buddhism"},"m":[{"p":[[34.62,112.45],[39.03,125.75],[36.28,126.91],[34.69,135.8]],"t":"To Korea and Japan"}]},
 {"t":"640: Nalanda, a school for Asia","text":"In India the great monastery of Nalanda taught thousands of monks from across Asia. The Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang studied there in the 630s and described its halls and libraries. Its teachers shaped the Mahayana schools that spread north and east. In the south and in Sri Lanka the older Theravada tradition held on.","y":640,"r":["sa","ea","ca"],"l":["religion","ideas"],"item":"Nalanda","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Nalanda mahavihara","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nalanda_mahavihara"}},
 {"t":"779: Tibet","text":"Tibet took its Buddhism mainly from India. King Trisong Detsen invited the Indian teachers Shantarakshita and Padmasambhava, and Samye, the first Tibetan monastery, was founded in about 779. The king made Buddhism the faith of his court. Tibetan monks translated the Indian texts with care, and many survive today only in Tibetan.","y":779,"r":["ca","sa"],"l":["religion"],"item":"Buddhism made state religion","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Tibetan Buddhism","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tibetan_Buddhism"},"m":[{"p":[[25.14,85.44],[27.7,85.32],[29.33,91.5]],"t":"Teachers from India"}]},
 {"t":"800: By sea to Java","text":"Ships carried Buddhism across the Bay of Bengal. The sea kingdom of Srivijaya on Sumatra was a centre of study by the late 600s. Around 800 the rulers of central Java built Borobudur, a stone hill of terraces carved with the Buddha's life. On the mainland, Theravada Buddhism from Sri Lanka later became the faith of Burma, Thailand and Cambodia.","y":800,"r":["se","sa"],"l":["religion"],"item":"Borobudur built","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Borobudur","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur"},"m":[{"p":[[22.3,87.92],[5.7,100.4],[-2.99,104.76],[-7.61,110.2]],"t":"By sea"}]},
 {"t":"1200: Fading at home","text":"In its homeland Buddhism faded. Over centuries Hindu devotion drew away its followers and its royal patrons. Around 1200 Turkic armies destroyed the great monasteries of Bihar, Nalanda among them, and monks fled to Nepal and Tibet. By then the faith was rooted from Sri Lanka to Japan, and it has lasted there to this day.","y":1206,"r":["sa","ca"],"l":["religion"],"item":"Delhi Sultanate","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Decline of Buddhism in the Indian subcontinent","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decline_of_Buddhism_in_the_Indian_subcontinent"},"m":[{"p":[[25.14,85.44],[27.7,85.32],[29.65,91.1]],"t":"Monks flee north"}]}]},
{"id":"islam","title":"The spread of Islam","sub":"From Mecca to Spain, Mali and Java, 622 to 1500","stops":[
 {"t":"622: The Hijra","text":"In 622 Muhammad and his followers left Mecca, where they were persecuted, for the town of Medina, about 200 miles north. This move, the Hijra, is year one of the Muslim calendar. In Medina he led a community as well as a faith. By his death in 632 most of Arabia had accepted Islam.","y":622,"r":["me"],"l":["religion"],"item":"Hijra","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Hijrah","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hijrah"},"m":[{"p":[[21.42,39.83],[24.47,39.61]],"t":"The Hijra"}]},
 {"t":"650: Two empires fall back","text":"Within twenty years of his death Arab armies had taken Syria, Egypt, Iraq and Persia from the Byzantine and Sasanian empires. Both were worn out by a long war with each other. The conquerors mostly left Christians, Jews and Zoroastrians to their faiths in return for a tax. Most people became Muslim only over the next few centuries.","y":650,"r":["me","eu"],"l":["war"],"item":"Rashidun & Umayyad Caliphates","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Early Muslim conquests","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_Muslim_conquests"},"m":[{"p":[[24.47,39.61],[33.51,36.29]],"t":"Syria, 636"},{"p":[[24.47,39.61],[30.01,31.23]],"t":"Egypt, 641"},{"p":[[24.47,39.61],[33.09,44.58],[34.19,48.37]],"t":"Persia, 637 to 642"}]},
 {"t":"711: Spain and the Indus","text":"The Umayyad caliphs ruled from Damascus. In 711 an army of Berbers and Arabs crossed from Morocco into Spain and overthrew the Visigothic kingdom in a few years. In 732 a Frankish army stopped a raid near Tours in France. In 711 too, far to the east, another Arab army took Sindh on the Indus.","y":711,"r":["me","eu","sa"],"l":["war"],"item":"Umayyads invade Hispania","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Umayyad Caliphate","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Umayyad_Caliphate"},"m":[{"p":[[35.68,10.1],[35.76,-5.83],[37.89,-4.78],[47.39,0.69]],"t":"West, 711 to 732"},{"p":[[30.5,47.8],[24.86,67.0]],"t":"Sindh, 711"}]},
 {"t":"800: Baghdad","text":"In 750 the Abbasids overthrew the Umayyads, and in 762 they built a new capital, Baghdad. It became one of the largest cities in the world. Scholars there translated Greek, Persian and Indian works into Arabic and added their own in mathematics, medicine and astronomy. The word algebra comes from a book written in Baghdad by al-Khwarizmi.","y":800,"r":["me","ca"],"l":["ideas"],"item":"Baghdad founded","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: House of Wisdom","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Wisdom"}},
 {"t":"1100: Traders carry it further","text":"Beyond the caliphate, traders spread Islam more than armies did. Muslim merchants sailing with the monsoon settled along the East African coast, and the Swahili towns built mosques of coral stone. Across the Sahara, camel caravans brought the faith to the rulers of the gold kingdoms. The ruler of the Maldives converted in 1153.","y":1100,"r":["af","me","sa"],"l":["trade","religion"],"item":"Swahili city-states","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Spread of Islam","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spread_of_Islam"},"m":[{"p":[[27.67,52.34],[12.8,45.03],[2.04,45.34],[-8.96,39.5]],"t":"Monsoon trade"},{"p":[[31.28,-4.28],[17.4,-10.4],[15.77,-7.97]],"t":"Caravans"}]},
 {"t":"1324: A king's pilgrimage","text":"In 1324 Mansa Musa, the Muslim king of Mali, crossed the Sahara on pilgrimage to Mecca. He gave away so much gold in Cairo that, a writer there reported, its price stayed low for years. He came home with scholars and builders, and Timbuktu grew into a city of mosques and books.","y":1324,"r":["af","me"],"l":["religion"],"item":"Mansa Musa's pilgrimage","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Mansa Musa","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mansa_Musa"},"m":[{"p":[[11.38,-8.38],[16.77,-3.0],[27.0,0.2],[30.04,31.24],[21.42,39.83]],"t":"Mansa Musa, 1324"}]},
 {"t":"1420: Malacca and the islands","text":"Islam reached Southeast Asia by sea, with traders from Gujarat, south India and Arabia. A Muslim state stood on north Sumatra by about 1300. The ruler of Malacca, the great port on the strait between the Indian Ocean and the China seas, became Muslim in the early 1400s. From there the faith spread along the trade routes to Java and the spice islands.","y":1420,"r":["se","sa","me"],"l":["trade","religion"],"item":"Malacca Sultanate","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Spread of Islam in Indonesia","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spread_of_Islam_in_Indonesia"},"m":[{"p":[[22.3,72.6],[11.25,75.78],[5.1,97.2],[2.19,102.25],[-6.89,110.64],[0.79,127.38]],"t":"The traders' route"}]},
 {"t":"1453: Constantinople","text":"In 1453 the Ottoman sultan Mehmed II took Constantinople, the last piece of the Roman Empire in the east. By 1500 Muslim rulers governed from Morocco to Bengal, and Muslim traders linked West Africa to China. In Spain the story ran the other way. Granada, the last Muslim kingdom there, fell in 1492.","y":1453,"r":["me","eu"],"l":["war"],"item":"Fall of Constantinople","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Fall of Constantinople","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_Constantinople"}}]},
{"id":"plagues","title":"Plagues that changed the world","sub":"Seven epidemics and the end of smallpox, 165 to 1977","stops":[
 {"t":"165: The Antonine Plague","text":"In 165 Roman soldiers came home from a war in Mesopotamia and brought a new disease with them. The doctor Galen described fever and a rash of black blisters, so it was probably smallpox, though nobody can be sure. It spread through the empire for fifteen years or more. Estimates of the dead run from five to ten million.","y":165,"r":["eu","me"],"l":["disaster"],"item":"Antonine Plague","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Antonine Plague","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonine_Plague"},"m":[{"p":[[33.09,44.52],[36.2,36.16],[37.94,27.34],[41.9,12.5]],"t":"Home from the war"}]},
 {"t":"541: The Plague of Justinian","text":"In 541 plague appeared at Pelusium in Egypt. It reached Constantinople the next spring on the grain ships. The historian Procopius, who was there, wrote that at its peak it killed thousands a day. DNA from graves shows it was bubonic plague. It came back in waves for two centuries and weakened the empire that Justinian was trying to rebuild.","y":541,"r":["eu","me"],"l":["disaster"],"item":"Plague of Justinian","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Plague of Justinian","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plague_of_Justinian"},"m":[{"p":[[31.04,32.55],[31.2,29.9],[41.01,28.98]],"t":"With the grain ships"},{"p":[[31.2,29.9],[37.07,15.29],[41.9,12.5]],"t":"West"}]},
 {"t":"1348: After the Black Death","text":"The same disease came back in 1346 and killed between a third and a half of Europe in five years. Afterwards workers were scarce. Wages rose, and laws that tried to hold them down helped set off revolts. In 1377 the port of Ragusa made ships from infected places wait a month offshore. Venice made it forty days, in Italian quaranta giorni, hence quarantine.","y":1348,"r":["eu","me"],"l":["disaster"],"item":"Black Death","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Consequences of the Black Death","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consequences_of_the_Black_Death"}},
 {"t":"1520: Smallpox in Mexico","text":"The peoples of the Americas had never met smallpox, measles or flu. Smallpox landed in Mexico in 1520 with a Spanish expedition. It swept through Tenochtitlan while the city was fighting Cortés, and killed the ruler Cuitláhuac. Over the next century, war, forced labour and wave after wave of disease cut the population of the Americas by perhaps nine tenths.","y":1520,"r":["am","eu"],"l":["disaster"],"item":"Smallpox strikes the Aztecs","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: History of smallpox","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_smallpox"},"m":[{"p":[[23.1,-82.4],[19.2,-96.13],[19.43,-99.13]],"t":"Smallpox, 1520"},{"p":[[19.43,-99.13],[14.6,-90.5],[9.0,-79.5],[-13.5,-72.0]],"t":"South, ahead of the Spanish"}]},
 {"t":"1796: Jenner's vaccine","text":"In 1796 the English doctor Edward Jenner scratched pus from a cowpox sore into the arm of an eight-year-old boy, and the boy proved immune to smallpox. The word vaccine comes from vacca, Latin for cow. From 1803 a Spanish expedition carried it to the Americas and the Philippines, kept alive by passing it from one orphan boy's arm to the next.","y":1796,"r":["eu","am","se"],"l":["ideas"],"item":"Jenner's smallpox vaccine","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Balmis Expedition","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balmis_Expedition"},"m":[{"p":[[43.37,-8.4],[28.47,-16.25],[18.47,-66.1],[19.2,-96.13]],"t":"Balmis expedition, 1803"}]},
 {"t":"1817: Cholera leaves Bengal","text":"Cholera had long been known in the Ganges delta. In 1817 it broke out and travelled with armies and trading ships to Java, China and Persia. A second wave reached Europe in 1831. In 1854 the London doctor John Snow traced an outbreak to one water pump. Cities then built sewers and clean water supplies, and those saved more lives than any medicine of the time.","y":1817,"r":["sa","se","ea","me","eu"],"l":["disaster"],"item":"Cholera pandemic from Bengal","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: 1817–1824 cholera pandemic","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1817–1824_cholera_pandemic"},"m":[{"p":[[22.57,88.36],[6.9,79.85],[2.19,102.25],[-6.2,106.8]],"t":"East by sea"},{"p":[[22.57,88.36],[18.95,72.83],[27.18,56.27],[32.6,51.67]],"t":"West to Persia"}]},
 {"t":"1918: The great flu","text":"The flu of 1918 went round the world in months, carried by troop ships and trains, and killed perhaps fifty million people. In November a ship from Auckland brought it to Western Samoa, and about a fifth of the people there died. In American Samoa, forty miles away, the governor closed the ports. Nobody there died of it.","y":1918,"r":["se","eu","am"],"l":["disaster"],"item":"1918 flu in Samoa","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: 1918 flu pandemic","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1918_flu_pandemic"},"m":[{"p":[[-36.85,174.76],[-18.14,178.44]],"t":"The Talune sails for Samoa"}]},
 {"t":"1977: The last case of smallpox","text":"Smallpox killed perhaps 300 million people in the twentieth century alone. In 1967 the World Health Organization set out to end it. Teams tracked each outbreak and vaccinated everyone around it. In October 1977 Ali Maow Maalin, a hospital cook in Merca, Somalia, caught the last natural case, and he recovered. No other human disease has been wiped out.","y":1977,"r":["af"],"l":["ideas"],"item":"Last natural case of smallpox","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Eradication of smallpox","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eradication_of_smallpox"}}]},
{"id":"african-kingdoms","title":"Kingdoms and trade in Africa","sub":"Farmers, gold, salt and the ocean trades, 1000 BCE to 1600","stops":[
 {"t":"1000 BCE: The Bantu expansion","text":"About 3,000 years ago farmers who spoke early Bantu languages began to move out of the borderland of today's Nigeria and Cameroon. Over many generations their descendants spread east to the great lakes and south to the tip of the continent. They carried yams, later cattle and iron tools. Today some 350 million people speak the 500 or so Bantu languages.","y":-1000,"r":["af"],"l":["migration"],"item":"Bantu expansion","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Bantu expansion","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bantu_expansion"},"m":[{"p":[[6.0,10.5],[1.0,18.0],[-2.0,30.0],[-12.0,34.0],[-25.0,31.0]],"t":"East, then south"},{"p":[[6.0,10.5],[-4.5,13.5],[-12.0,16.0],[-18.0,22.0]],"t":"Down the west"}]},
 {"t":"330: Aksum","text":"Aksum, in the highlands of Ethiopia and Eritrea, grew rich on Red Sea trade. From its port of Adulis it sent ivory and gold to Egypt, Arabia and India. It struck its own gold coins, which only a few states then did. About 330 its king Ezana became a Christian, and the Ethiopian church dates from his reign.","y":330,"r":["af","me","sa"],"l":["trade"],"item":"Aksum adopts Christianity","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Aksum","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Aksum"},"m":[{"p":[[14.13,38.72],[15.26,39.66],[12.6,43.4],[12.5,54.0],[11.0,75.5]],"t":"To India"},{"p":[[15.26,39.66],[23.9,35.5],[31.2,29.9]],"t":"To Egypt"}]},
 {"t":"800: Gold for salt","text":"South of the Sahara, the kings of Ghana, or Wagadu, controlled the trade in gold. The gold came from fields further south. Camel caravans brought salt from mines in the desert, and it was traded for the gold. The geographer al-Bakri, writing in 1068, said the king could raise 200,000 soldiers, and that his dogs wore collars of gold.","y":800,"r":["af","me"],"l":["trade"],"item":"Ghana (Wagadu)","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Ghana Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghana_Empire"},"m":[{"p":[[31.28,-4.28],[23.6,-5.0],[15.77,-7.97]],"t":"Salt goes south"},{"p":[[12.5,-11.5],[15.77,-7.97],[31.28,-4.28]],"t":"Gold goes north"}]},
 {"t":"1300: Great Zimbabwe","text":"In the south, Shona-speaking rulers built Great Zimbabwe between about 1100 and 1450. Its walls are granite blocks laid without mortar, up to 36 feet high. Between 10,000 and 18,000 people lived there. Its wealth came from cattle and from gold, which went to the coast at Sofala and on to Kilwa. Chinese porcelain and Persian glass have been found in the ruins.","y":1300,"r":["af"],"l":["trade"],"item":"Great Zimbabwe","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Great Zimbabwe","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Zimbabwe"},"m":[{"p":[[-20.27,30.93],[-20.15,34.72],[-8.96,39.5]],"t":"Gold to Sofala and Kilwa"},{"p":[[-8.96,39.5],[12.8,45.03],[22.3,72.6]],"t":"Indian Ocean trade"}]},
 {"t":"1470: Songhai and Timbuktu","text":"Mali had replaced Ghana, and in the 1400s Songhai replaced Mali. Its king Sunni Ali took Timbuktu in 1468, and it became the largest state West Africa had seen. Timbuktu was a city of scholars, and its families kept many thousands of handwritten books. In 1591 a Moroccan army with guns crossed the desert and broke the empire.","y":1470,"r":["af","me"],"l":["ideas"],"item":"Songhai Empire","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Songhai Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Songhai_Empire"},"m":[{"p":[[16.27,-0.04],[16.77,-3.0],[13.9,-4.55]],"t":"Songhai on the Niger"},{"p":[[31.63,-8.0],[23.6,-5.0],[16.77,-3.0]],"t":"Morocco invades, 1591"}]},
 {"t":"1491: Kongo meets Portugal","text":"Portuguese ships reached the mouth of the Congo River in 1483. In 1491 the king of Kongo, Nzinga a Nkuwu, was baptised. His son Afonso I ruled as a Christian king for over thirty years and wrote letters to the king of Portugal as an equal. In 1526 he complained that Portuguese traders were seizing his people, even nobles, as slaves.","y":1491,"r":["af","eu"],"l":["religion"],"item":"Kongo king baptised","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Kongo","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Kongo"},"m":[{"p":[[38.72,-9.14],[15.0,-23.5],[4.0,-8.0],[-6.0,12.4],[-6.27,14.25]],"t":"Portuguese ships"}]},
 {"t":"1500: Benin","text":"Benin, in the forest of today's southern Nigeria, was ringed by miles of earth walls and ditches. Its guild of casters made brass heads and plaques for the palace, recording kings, courtiers and Portuguese visitors. Benin sold pepper, ivory and cloth to the Portuguese and for long periods limited the sale of slaves. In 1897 a British force looted the bronzes.","y":1500,"r":["af","eu"],"l":["arts"],"item":"Benin Bronzes","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Benin Bronzes","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benin_Bronzes"}},
 {"t":"1526: The Atlantic slave trade","text":"From the early 1500s European ships carried enslaved Africans straight across the Atlantic. Over the next 350 years about 12.5 million people were put on the ships, and about 10.7 million lived to land. Most went to Brazil and the Caribbean to grow sugar. The trade enriched some African rulers and traders, and it emptied or wrecked whole regions.","y":1526,"r":["af","am","eu"],"l":["trade","migration"],"item":"Atlantic slave trade","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Atlantic slave trade","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_slave_trade"},"m":[{"p":[[-8.84,13.23],[-10.0,-15.0],[-12.97,-38.5]],"t":"To Brazil"},{"p":[[6.3,2.4],[10.0,-30.0],[18.0,-72.0]],"t":"To the Caribbean"}]}]},
{"id":"pacific","title":"The settling of the Pacific","sub":"From Taiwan to Rapa Nui and New Zealand by canoe, 2200 BCE to 1976","stops":[
 {"t":"2200 BCE: Out of Taiwan","text":"The story begins in Taiwan. About 2200 BCE farmers there, speakers of Austronesian languages, crossed to the Philippines by boat. Over the next thousand years their descendants spread through the islands of Southeast Asia. They carried rice, pigs, dogs and chickens, and they built the outrigger canoe, which stays upright in open sea. Every Pacific voyage after that began with this boat.","y":-2200,"r":["se","ea"],"l":["migration"],"item":"Austronesians reach the Philippines","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Austronesian peoples","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austronesian_peoples"},"m":[{"p":[[23.7,121.0],[16.5,121.3],[7.0,125.5],[1.0,124.8]],"t":"South from Taiwan"}]},
 {"t":"1300 BCE: The Lapita voyagers","text":"Around 1500 BCE a people who made stamped pottery, called Lapita, appeared in the islands off New Guinea. Within a few centuries they had sailed east past the Solomon Islands into empty ocean, and reached Fiji, Tonga and Samoa by about 900 BCE. Nobody had lived there before. Their descendants in Tonga and Samoa became the Polynesians.","y":-1300,"r":["se"],"l":["migration"],"item":"Lapita voyagers","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Lapita culture","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapita_culture"},"m":[{"p":[[-4.2,152.2],[-9.5,160.2],[-16.0,167.5],[-17.8,178.0]],"t":"Lapita, east to Fiji and beyond"}]},
 {"t":"500: West to Madagascar","text":"Other Austronesians sailed west. People from Borneo crossed the whole Indian Ocean and settled Madagascar, off Africa, at some point between about 350 BCE and 550 CE. The date is still argued over. The Malagasy language is closest to a language spoken in southern Borneo, 4,000 miles away. They brought rice, bananas and outrigger canoes with them.","y":500,"r":["se","af","sa"],"l":["migration"],"item":"Settlement of Madagascar","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Malagasy people","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malagasy_people"},"m":[{"p":[[-3.3,114.6],[-6.1,105.9],[-7.3,72.4],[-15.7,46.3]],"t":"Borneo to Madagascar"}]},
 {"t":"1050: The long pause ends","text":"For almost two thousand years nobody settled further east than Samoa. Then, from about 1000 CE, Polynesians moved fast. New dating puts the Society Islands at about 1025 to 1120, and the Marquesas, Hawaii and Rapa Nui at about 1190 to 1290. Navigators found islands by the stars, the swells, and the birds that fly out to sea and home each day.","y":1050,"r":["se"],"l":["migration"],"item":"Settlement of East Polynesia","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Polynesian navigation","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynesian_navigation"},"m":[{"p":[[-13.8,-172.1],[-21.23,-159.78],[-17.65,-149.45]],"t":"To the Society Islands"},{"p":[[-17.65,-149.45],[-9.0,-139.5],[20.8,-156.3]],"t":"To Hawaii"},{"p":[[-17.65,-149.45],[-23.1,-134.97],[-27.12,-109.36]],"t":"To Rapa Nui"}]},
 {"t":"1100: A plant from South America","text":"The sweet potato comes from South America, and yet Polynesians were growing it centuries before Europeans arrived. The oldest remains, in the Cook Islands, date to about 1000 to 1200. The Polynesian word kumara is close to kumar, a Quechua word from the Andes. Most scholars think Polynesian sailors reached the American coast and came back with it.","y":1100,"r":["se","am"],"l":["food"],"item":"Sweet potato reaches Polynesia","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Pre-Columbian transoceanic contact theories","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pre-Columbian_transoceanic_contact_theories"},"m":[{"p":[[-12.0,-77.1],[-9.0,-139.5],[-21.23,-159.78]],"t":"Sweet potato"}]},
 {"t":"1280: Aotearoa","text":"New Zealand was the last large land on Earth that people settled. Canoes from East Polynesia arrived between about 1250 and 1300, and their people became the Māori. They found forests full of moa, flightless birds up to twelve feet tall. Within about a century the moa had been hunted out, and the Māori turned to farming, fishing and fortified villages.","y":1280,"r":["se"],"l":[],"item":"Māori settle Aotearoa","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Māori history","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Māori_history"}},
 {"t":"1400: The statues of Rapa Nui","text":"Rapa Nui, or Easter Island, lies 1,200 miles from the nearest inhabited island. Its people carved almost a thousand stone figures, the moai, most of them from one quarry, and moved them miles to platforms by the shore. The largest one set up weighs about 80 tons. They were images of ancestors, and nearly all face inland, toward the villages.","y":1400,"r":["se"],"l":["arts"],"item":"Moai of Rapa Nui","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Moai","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moai"}},
 {"t":"1769: A navigator on a British ship","text":"In 1769 James Cook reached Tahiti. A priest and navigator named Tupaia joined his ship and guided it through the islands. He drew a chart of more than seventy islands, some of them weeks away by sail. Cook was struck that people from Hawaii to New Zealand spoke related languages. He guessed, correctly, that one people had settled them all.","y":1769,"r":["se","eu"],"l":["people"],"item":"Tupaia","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Tupaia (navigator)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tupaia_(navigator)"},"m":[{"p":[[-17.65,-149.45],[-16.8,-151.4],[-22.47,-151.34]],"t":"Endeavour, 1769"}]},
 {"t":"1976: Proof by canoe","text":"By the 1900s some writers claimed the Pacific had been settled by accident, by canoes blown off course. In 1976 the Hōkūleʻa, a copy of a voyaging canoe, sailed 2,500 miles from Hawaii to Tahiti with no instruments. Mau Piailug, a navigator from Micronesia, steered by stars and swells. The voyage showed the old method worked, and it helped revive Polynesian sailing.","y":1976,"r":["se"],"l":["ideas"],"item":"Hōkūleʻa","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Hōkūleʻa","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hōkūleʻa"},"m":[{"p":[[20.8,-156.3],[2.0,-150.0],[-17.65,-149.45]],"t":"Hōkūleʻa, 1976"}]}]},
{"id":"americas","title":"Cities and empires of the Americas","sub":"From the first towns of Peru to Spanish silver, 2600 BCE to 1545","stops":[
 {"t":"2600 BCE: Caral","text":"The oldest known towns in the Americas stood in the dry river valleys of the coast of Peru. At Caral, people were building stone platform mounds by about 2600 BCE, when the pyramids of Egypt were going up. They had no pottery and no writing. They grew cotton for fishing nets, and traded it for dried fish from the coast.","y":-2600,"r":["am"],"l":[],"item":"Caral–Supe","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Caral–Supe civilization","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caral–Supe_civilization"}},
 {"t":"1200 BCE: The Olmec","text":"On the hot Gulf coast of Mexico, the Olmec built the first large centres of Mesoamerica, at San Lorenzo and then La Venta. They carved heads of rulers from basalt, up to eleven feet high, and dragged the stone fifty miles or more. Later peoples of Mexico kept much that is first seen here: the ball game, jade carving and temple mounds.","y":-1200,"r":["am"],"l":["arts"],"item":"Olmec centres","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Olmecs","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olmecs"}},
 {"t":"400: Teotihuacan","text":"By 400 CE Teotihuacan, in the highlands of Mexico, held about 100,000 people. That made it one of the largest cities in the world. It was laid out on a grid along a wide avenue, with two great pyramids. Nobody knows what its people called it or what language they spoke. In 378 its soldiers appear at Tikal, 600 miles away, where a new king took power.","y":400,"r":["am"],"l":[],"item":"Teotihuacan","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Teotihuacan","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teotihuacan"},"m":[{"p":[[19.69,-98.84],[17.5,-94.5],[17.22,-89.62]],"t":"To Tikal, 378"}]},
 {"t":"700: The Maya cities","text":"The Maya lived in dozens of rival city-states, with Tikal and Calakmul the strongest. They had a full writing system, the only one known in the Americas, and a calendar that counted days over thousands of years. They wrote a sign for zero. Between about 800 and 900 the cities of the southern lowlands were abandoned, after drought and war. The cities of the north carried on.","y":700,"r":["am"],"l":["ideas"],"item":"Maya city-states","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Maya civilization","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maya_civilization"}},
 {"t":"1100: Cahokia","text":"Near today's St. Louis, on the Mississippi, stood Cahokia. Around 1100 it had perhaps 15,000 people and more than a hundred earth mounds. The largest, Monks Mound, covers fourteen acres at its base. Its people grew maize and traded for shell from the Gulf and copper from the Great Lakes. By 1350 the city was empty, and the reasons are still debated.","y":1100,"r":["am"],"l":[],"item":"Cahokia & Mississippian towns","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Cahokia","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cahokia"},"m":[{"p":[[29.5,-90.0],[35.1,-90.1],[38.66,-90.06]],"t":"Shell from the Gulf"},{"p":[[47.0,-88.5],[41.9,-87.7],[38.66,-90.06]],"t":"Copper from the lakes"}]},
 {"t":"1325: Tenochtitlan","text":"The Mexica, later called Aztecs, founded Tenochtitlan on an island in Lake Texcoco in 1325. They built fields on the lake bed and causeways to the shore. In 1428 they joined two other cities in an alliance that came to tax most of central Mexico. By 1500 the city held about 200,000 people, more than any city in Spain.","y":1325,"r":["am"],"l":[],"item":"Tenochtitlan founded","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Tenochtitlan","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tenochtitlan"}},
 {"t":"1470: The Inca roads","text":"From Cusco the Inca conquered the Andes in less than a century. They held it together with about 25,000 miles of road, with rope bridges and storehouses along the way. Relay runners could carry a message 150 miles in a day. They had no writing. Officials kept the accounts of the empire on quipu, cords with knots tied in them.","y":1470,"r":["am"],"l":["infra"],"item":"Inca road system","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Inca road system","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inca_road_system"},"m":[{"p":[[-0.22,-78.51],[-7.16,-78.51],[-13.52,-71.97]],"t":"Quito to Cusco"},{"p":[[-13.52,-71.97],[-16.5,-68.15],[-24.8,-65.4],[-33.45,-70.67]],"t":"South to Chile"}]},
 {"t":"1521: The Spanish conquests","text":"In 1519 Hernán Cortés landed in Mexico with a few hundred men. He won tens of thousands of allies among peoples the Aztecs had taxed, above all the Tlaxcalans. Smallpox struck the city during the fighting, and Tenochtitlan fell in August 1521. In 1532 Francisco Pizarro seized the Inca ruler Atahualpa at Cajamarca, just as an Inca civil war was ending.","y":1521,"r":["am","eu"],"l":["war"],"item":"Fall of Tenochtitlan","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spanish_conquest_of_the_Aztec_Empire"},"m":[{"p":[[23.1,-82.4],[19.2,-96.13],[19.32,-98.24],[19.43,-99.13]],"t":"Cortés, 1519 to 1521"},{"p":[[9.0,-79.5],[-3.57,-80.45],[-7.16,-78.51]],"t":"Pizarro, 1532"}]},
 {"t":"1545: Silver for the world","text":"In 1545 the Spanish found a mountain of silver ore at Potosí, high in the Andes. Forced Andean labour mined it. By 1600 Potosí had more people than Madrid. The silver crossed the Atlantic to Seville, and from 1565 it also crossed the Pacific from Acapulco to Manila in the Philippines, where it paid for Chinese silk and porcelain. For the first time trade ran right round the world.","y":1545,"r":["am","eu","se","ea"],"l":["trade"],"item":"Potosí silver","src":{"t":"Wikipedia: Potosí","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potosí"},"m":[{"p":[[-19.58,-65.75],[-12.05,-77.1],[9.0,-79.5],[23.1,-82.4],[37.39,-5.99]],"t":"To Seville"}]}]}
];
